all_rationales:
Punctuation marks indicate when and where sentences end and begin again inside paragraphs. Punctuation helps readers follow along smoothly from sentence to sentence without being interrupted by new beginnings every time there's another idea presented.
Commas separate items (words) joined together into compound nouns like hamburger cheeseburger dogfish catfish fisherwoman.
Semicolons join independent statements about different things going on simultaneously; they're often easier read if one statement follows quickly after the other rather than waiting until later down-page before continuing onto yet-another-idea.
Colons introduce lists followed up immediately afterward, such as the ingredients needed to bake cookies;
Exclamation points emphasize strong feelings,
问号 asks questions people may be too polite to voice directly out loud;
Quotation marks show off quotes someone else has said nicely phrased already!);
Dashes interrupt their surroundings and set them apart from each other neatly;
Brackets tell us information we might have left out otherwise (!).
So think carefully whether your reader needs all these kinds o' puncs just because he knows some good-looking fonts? If those letters could talk...
